WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:04.930
We’re in a heatwave here, so er I’m in
the woods.

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:09.240
Hello everyone this is the map of superconductivity,
where, as ever, I’ve broken down all the

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:15.070
important parts of the subject into a big
picture to get you up to speed quickly and

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:17.030
as clearly as possible.

00:00:17.030 --> 00:00:21.121
Superconductors are materials which, when
you cool them down to a low temperature, they

00:00:21.121 --> 00:00:23.740
lose their electrical resistance.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:29.710
They also have some interesting magnetic properties
which allow them to almost magically float,

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:33.060
but it’s not magic, it’s just plain old
quantum mechanics.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:38.140
We’ll look at the different kinds of superconductors,
their properties, the theory behind them,

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:43.610
their applications in the real world, and
the future avenues of research and technology.

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:48.540
And as we go, see if you can keep count of
all the nobel prizes.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:51.240
First I need to tell you about magnetic induction.

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:55.690
If you have a conducting material, which is
a material that has electrons that can move

00:00:55.690 --> 00:01:01.350
around freely, like a piece of metal at room
temperature, and you move a permanent magnet

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:06.400
near to it, these electrons feel this changing
magnetic field, they feel a force from it,

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:10.030
and start moving in a circle called an eddy
current.

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:12.740
This is called magnetic induction.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:18.220
In a normal metal this current dies away quickly
because the material has electrical resistance:

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:23.530
the moving electrons bang into the atoms and
stop moving, giving up their energy to vibrations

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:26.930
in the atomic lattice warming it up slightly.

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:31.810
But if you do the same thing to a superconductor,
because they have got zero electrical resistance,

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:36.160
the eddy current will never stop flowing and
will carry on circulating forever.

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:41.390
Like, age of the universe forever according
to the theory, and experimentally we’ve

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:46.210
seen currents losing no energy over twenty
five years.

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:50.650
Zero electrical resistance also means you
can pass a direct current through a superconductor

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:53.210
without it losing any energy at all.

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:57.909
That’s cool, but they have another important
property to do with magnetic fields called

00:01:57.909 --> 00:02:00.430
the Meissner effect.

00:02:00.430 --> 00:02:02.939
Superconductors expel any magnetic field inside
them.

00:02:02.939 --> 00:02:07.509
You know how I said that magnetic fields induce
electrical currents in conductors.

00:02:07.509 --> 00:02:14.230
Well the opposite is also true, any electrical
current creates a magnetic field.

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:18.370
If a superconductor is in a magnetic field,
it gets a load of eddy currents which create

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:24.439
their own magnetic fields that exactly cancel
out and expel the original magnetic field.

00:02:24.439 --> 00:02:28.660
This is a quantum effect and doesn’t happen
in normal conductors.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:33.400
So those are the two main features of superconductors,
zero electrical resistance and the Meissner

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:34.400
effect.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:39.209
And when they were discovered in the early
nineteen hundreds physicists were like whoa!

00:02:39.209 --> 00:02:43.970
and then since then they have investigated
more and made a load of useful technology

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:46.670
out of them which we’ll look at in a bit.

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:51.549
But first we need to look at exactly what
conditions are needed for a superconductor

00:02:51.549 --> 00:02:52.779
to superconduct.

00:02:52.779 --> 00:02:57.739
There are three conditions you need for a
superconductor, low temperatures, small enough

00:02:57.739 --> 00:03:03.340
magnetic fields and small enough electrical
currents although these two are kind of the

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:04.340
same thing.

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:09.430
The specific temperature and magnetic field
that breaks superconductivity depends on the

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:10.629
material.

00:03:10.629 --> 00:03:14.939
The first superconductors that were studied
were pure elements like mercury, aluminium

00:03:14.939 --> 00:03:20.340
or niobium, and physicists discovered that
not all of the elements superconduct, here

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:22.079
are the ones that do.

00:03:22.079 --> 00:03:30.129
For each material as you cool them down they
undergo a sharp transition temperature where

00:03:30.129 --> 00:03:33.849
they suddenly start superconducting at a sharp
phase transition.

00:03:33.849 --> 00:03:39.150
Then when they are in the superconducting
state if you apply a larger and larger magnetic

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:44.999
field or larger and larger current they have
a critical field or critical current where

00:03:44.999 --> 00:03:50.370
they suddenly stop superconducting and go
back through the phase transition to a normal

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:54.829
conductor even if they are below the transition
temperature.

00:03:54.829 --> 00:03:59.439
Even if all of this is new you already know
about phase transitions, because this is what

00:03:59.439 --> 00:04:04.430
happens to water when it freezes or when it
boils.

00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:09.670
These are phase transitions too, but those
are phase transitions in the material properties,

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:14.949
whereas the superconducting phase transitions
are transitions in the electronic properties.

00:04:14.949 --> 00:04:21.010
But it all comes down to what is the configuration
of stuff which minimises the overall energy,

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:23.480
known as the gibbs gree energy.

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:29.080
Ifa material will be in a lower energy by
freezing into a solid, or turning into a superconductor,

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:30.700
that’s what it will do.

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:35.160
Anyway, as any fan of physics knows, when
we have phase transitions we’re gunna have,

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:36.790
say it with me, phase diagrams!

00:04:36.790 --> 00:04:43.130
These are very handy graphs because they show
us what state your thing will be in when you

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:45.880
change some global parameters.

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:50.350
This is a phase diagram for the state of water
when you change temperature and pressure which

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:52.380
you might have seen before.

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:56.810
And here is the analogous example for the
superconducting state and normal conducting

00:04:56.810 --> 00:05:01.830
state for different temperatures and magnetic
fields of a superconductor.

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:07.130
This also changes with pressure as well, but
I’m not plotting that here because it’d

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:13.160
need a 3D plot, and superconductivity at pressure
is a bit of a research niche, the vast majority

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:16.730
of superconductors are used at normal pressure.

00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:21.880
Each superconductor has got its own unique
phase diagram and filling them in kept a bunch

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:27.190
of experimental physicists happily busy for
years as they filled in all the points.

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:31.090
And they didn’t just stick to the elements,
they started looking at compound materials,

00:05:31.090 --> 00:05:35.710
of which there are an infinite amount, and
the search for new superconductors has been

00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:40.390
going on ever since in an attempt to find
materials with higher and higher transition

00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:46.580
temperatures with the goal of one day finding
a room temperature superconductor and making

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:47.740
tonnes of cash.

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:52.280
Here’s a plot of the discovery of higher
and higher temperature superconductors, and

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:55.930
along the way they have made some startling
discoveries.

00:05:55.930 --> 00:06:00.680
First of all, in 1935 they discovered type-II
superconductors which behave differently to

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:06.510
the superconductors I’ve described so far,
which were then called type-I superconductors.

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:10.291
Type-II superconductors behave differently
to type-I superconductors when they are in

00:06:10.291 --> 00:06:12.030
a magnetic field.

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:15.910
Here’s a phase diagram of a type-II superconductor.

00:06:15.910 --> 00:06:20.310
Down here it still behaves just like a type-I
superconductor, but then they have an intermediate

00:06:20.310 --> 00:06:25.240
state where they do let the magnetic field
penetrate them, but only in specific points

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:31.150
in a formation called a magnetic field vortex,
or vortices for short.

00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:37.300
Here the bulk of the material is still superconducting,
but at these thread like vortices the material

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:42.680
is a normal conductor, the magnetic field
goes all the way through in a certain small

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:48.690
amount called a magnetic flux quanta, and
each one is surrounded by a swirling supercurrent,

00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:52.530
which is the name for the electrical current
in a superconductor.

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:57.250
I just threw a load of terminology at you
there, so if you are confused just think of

00:06:57.250 --> 00:07:00.900
them as a load of sausages.

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:04.400
And you’ll be pleased to hear that these
are the only two kinds of superconductors

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:05.670
we’ve found so far.

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:12.190
There is a kind of type-1.5 superconductor,
but I can’t be bothered to talk about them

00:07:12.190 --> 00:07:13.190
here.

00:07:13.190 --> 00:07:14.190
Wikipedia.

00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:17.840
The next big bombshell to hit the exciting
world of superconductivity research was in

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:24.420
1986 when researchers made a superconductor
made of ceramic which was an insulator before

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:26.240
being cooled down.

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:30.580
This then led to the discovery of a bunch
of high-temperature superconductors.

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:33.930
Although high-temperature is a little bit
of a misnomer, because they still have to

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:39.730
be cooled way below zero with cryogenic liquids
but now you can make a thing superconduct

00:07:39.730 --> 00:07:45.650
with just liquid nitrogen at 77 kelvin, which
is way cheaper than liquid helium which is

00:07:45.650 --> 00:07:48.060
what they had to use before.

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:51.840
These new superconductors are known as the
cuprates because they contain layers of copper

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:55.260
oxide, with a load of other stuff in there
as well.

00:07:55.260 --> 00:08:00.640
And there’s also many other types of superconductor
iron based superconductors called the pnictides,

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:06.420
pure carbon based superconductors called fullerenes
also known as organic superconductors and

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:08.830
there is others as well, but you get the picture.

00:08:08.830 --> 00:08:13.190
People have also squeezed materials between
diamond presses and discovered that many materials

00:08:13.190 --> 00:08:18.790
start superconducting when they are at very
high pressure and recently in 2020 a room

00:08:18.790 --> 00:08:25.169
temperature superconductor was discovered
called Carbonaceous sulfur hydride which superconducts

00:08:25.169 --> 00:08:30.530
at 15 celsius, but only under a huge amount
of pressure squeezed in a diamond press, so

00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:36.110
it’s not actually practically useful, but
still a landmark discovery.

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:38.069
Now on to the theory of superconductivity.

00:08:38.069 --> 00:08:45.050
It was not an easy job to figure out the underlying
theory of what is actually going on in superconducting

00:08:45.050 --> 00:08:46.809
materials at the scale of the electrons.

00:08:46.809 --> 00:08:51.360
What is the underlying process that allows
for zero resistance?

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:56.850
The first theory, Ginzberg-Landau theory predicted
properties of superconductors like, which

00:08:56.850 --> 00:09:02.180
would be type one and type two, but this was
superseded by a microscopic theory called

00:09:02.180 --> 00:09:05.759
BCS theory, named after the initials of the
creators.

00:09:05.759 --> 00:09:11.370
They figured out that, as electrons move through
the lattice of atoms, they attract the atoms

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:18.279
around them slightly, creating a local positive
charge which creates an attractive force between

00:09:18.279 --> 00:09:19.490
electrons.

00:09:19.490 --> 00:09:24.329
So electrons can interact with each other
through vibrations in the lattice called phonons

00:09:24.329 --> 00:09:29.509
and this allows them to pair up into a composite
entity known as a Cooper pair.

00:09:29.509 --> 00:09:33.499
Now, if you’ve watched my map of particle
physics you’ll know that electrons are spin

00:09:33.499 --> 00:09:39.309
half particles, and obey the pauli exclusion
principle so can’t exist in the same quantum

00:09:39.309 --> 00:09:40.309
states.

00:09:40.309 --> 00:09:44.610
But the cooper pairs behave like bosons, because
when you add their spins together you get

00:09:44.610 --> 00:09:47.220
spin zero or spin one.

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:52.459
Bosons can exist in the same quantum state
and so all the cooper pairs form a thing called

00:09:52.459 --> 00:09:57.670
a condensate which has special properties
including not being able to easily absorb

00:09:57.670 --> 00:10:01.180
kicks of energy, and so they flow without
resistance.

00:10:01.180 --> 00:10:03.529
This is known as an energy gap.

00:10:03.529 --> 00:10:08.490
That’s just a very quick explanation because
a full description would take a long time,

00:10:08.490 --> 00:10:10.680
but now you’ve got the basics.

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:15.029
What is interesting is this theory doesn’t
explain high temperature superconductivity

00:10:15.029 --> 00:10:19.690
because the attractive force from the phonons
don’t exist there.

00:10:19.690 --> 00:10:23.850
So they need some other attractive force between
electrons, and we don’t know where that

00:10:23.850 --> 00:10:26.470
would come from in high temperature superconductors.

00:10:26.470 --> 00:10:32.730
For this reason any superconductor that doesn’t
follow BCS theory is called an unconventional

00:10:32.730 --> 00:10:36.670
superconductor, and the ones that do are called
conventional superconductors.

00:10:36.670 --> 00:10:40.999
Despite a lot of work going into this high
temperature superconductivity is still one

00:10:40.999 --> 00:10:46.279
of the biggest unsolved mysteries in theoretical
condensed matter physics and you’ll probably

00:10:46.279 --> 00:10:48.329
win a Nobel prize when you figure it out.

00:10:48.329 --> 00:10:53.410
There are a load of technologies which use
superconductors.

00:10:53.410 --> 00:10:58.069
The most widespread use of superconductors
is to create large magnetic fields as you

00:10:58.069 --> 00:11:02.249
can circulate a lot of current in a loop without
burning any energy.

00:11:02.249 --> 00:11:08.230
This is what the big tube is in MRI machines,
that is basically coils and coils of superconducting

00:11:08.230 --> 00:11:11.920
material that is cooled down with liquid helium.

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:15.999
Superconducting magnets are also extensively
used in particle accelerators to bend and

00:11:15.999 --> 00:11:18.129
focus the beams of particles.

00:11:18.129 --> 00:11:23.339
They have been used in some tokamak reactors
to control the plasma in the nuclear fusion

00:11:23.339 --> 00:11:24.430
process.

00:11:24.430 --> 00:11:29.380
And also in other areas where you want to
control charged particles like mass spectrometers.

00:11:29.380 --> 00:11:34.449
There are also many kinds of quantum devices
which use superconductors.

00:11:34.449 --> 00:11:39.189
The most useful are josephson junctions, which
are small gaps between superconductors where

00:11:39.189 --> 00:11:43.870
the cooper pairs can still flow across the
gap through quantum tunneling, and so you

00:11:43.870 --> 00:11:48.639
get a continuous flow of current even with
no voltage applied.

00:11:48.639 --> 00:11:53.480
You can use this to set up superpositions
of current, and therefore superpositions of

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:58.980
magnetic field, where the current and magnetic
field are in the superposition state of flowing

00:11:58.980 --> 00:12:02.089
in both directions at the same time.

00:12:02.089 --> 00:12:06.420
These josephson junctions can be combined
in a specific formation called a superconducting

00:12:06.420 --> 00:12:12.779
quantum interference device or squid for short,
which is a phenomenally sensitive magnetic

00:12:12.779 --> 00:12:16.519
field detector, the best humanity has discovered.

00:12:16.519 --> 00:12:22.759
These are the detectors that see inside your
body in MRI and fMRI machines, they are also

00:12:22.759 --> 00:12:28.220
set the voltage standard in fundamental physics,
are used as efficient radio frequency antennas

00:12:28.220 --> 00:12:33.959
in research and in mobile phone masts, and
you can use the superposition of states created

00:12:33.959 --> 00:12:39.570
by the josephson junctions to make a tunable
qubit: which are the building blocks of superconducting

00:12:39.570 --> 00:12:41.709
quantum computers.

00:12:41.709 --> 00:12:47.089
This stuff is my professional background back
when I had a proper job, and I think quantum

00:12:47.089 --> 00:12:51.809
technology is absolutely fascinating, especially
the possible future technologies.

00:12:51.809 --> 00:12:54.380
So what does the future hold for superconductors?

00:12:54.380 --> 00:12:59.100
For a long time people have talked about building
transmission lines to transport electricity

00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:03.720
through superconductors to significantly reduce
the amount of energy that is lost.

00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:07.110
But to be cost effective it’d need to be
a superconductor that you don’t need to

00:13:07.110 --> 00:13:12.779
cool down very much, which can carry high
currents and is strong, we don’t have this

00:13:12.779 --> 00:13:18.100
yet, so these are the challenges, but it would
be cool to be able to ship electricity around

00:13:18.100 --> 00:13:20.860
the grid a lot more efficiently.

00:13:20.860 --> 00:13:25.619
You can also use superconductors for levitating
things like trains, but this potential has

00:13:25.619 --> 00:13:29.990
been around for a while so I’m not sure
there is a great need for it, but you know,

00:13:29.990 --> 00:13:31.470
it’s a thing that exists.

00:13:31.470 --> 00:13:36.499
A promising area is to make very efficient
superconducting motors or generators for things

00:13:36.499 --> 00:13:41.110
like wind turbines which could potentially
decrease the cost of the electricity they

00:13:41.110 --> 00:13:46.620
generate, so this would be really cool, to
make renewable energy even cheaper.

00:13:46.620 --> 00:13:51.370
On the quantum devices side, by far the most
exciting applications are the range of quantum

00:13:51.370 --> 00:13:53.970
computers built with superconducting qubits.

00:13:53.970 --> 00:13:58.779
Now, superconductors aren’t the only way
to build quantum computers, but google, IBM,

00:13:58.779 --> 00:14:04.459
D-Wave and others have built very advanced
superconducting quantum computers which could

00:14:04.459 --> 00:14:10.189
potentially be used to understand and find
new superconducting materials by simulating

00:14:10.189 --> 00:14:16.119
the quantum mechanics of them, something that
can’t currently be done with our most powerful

00:14:16.119 --> 00:14:17.119
supercomputers.

00:14:17.119 --> 00:14:21.480
So you could potentially use superconducting
qubits to figure out how high temperature

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:27.230
superconductivity actually works and then
perhaps use that to find a room temperature

00:14:27.230 --> 00:14:28.230
superconductor.

00:14:28.230 --> 00:14:33.819
In fact, on the research side, figuring out
the mechanism of high temperature superconductors

00:14:33.819 --> 00:14:40.100
and whether a room temperature and room pressure
superconductor can exist, would be fantastic.

00:14:40.100 --> 00:14:44.989
If we had a room temperature superconductor
it could potentially revolutionise all electronics

00:14:44.989 --> 00:14:49.890
from the power grid, to consumer electronics
as you’d be able to build zero resistance

00:14:49.890 --> 00:14:52.910
computers with way lower electricity consumption.

00:14:52.910 --> 00:14:57.810
But this all depends on the room temperature
superconductor also having a high critical

00:14:57.810 --> 00:15:03.610
current, and critical field, as well as having
material properties that make it easy to work

00:15:03.610 --> 00:15:05.550
with to fabricate chips.

00:15:05.550 --> 00:15:08.309
Okay so that’s the map of superconductivity.

00:15:08.309 --> 00:15:10.420
I hope it was informative.

00:15:10.420 --> 00:15:12.259
How many Nobel prizes did you count?

